u}\) . Characteristic lengths calculated for a half-inch-diameter aluminum rod. Longer periods penetrate farther into the rod but produce less phase change per unit length. Shorter periods produce more phase change per unit length but also attenuate more rapidly. The experimental period should give both a measurable amplitude and a resolvable phase difference across several sensors. You must generate your own version of this theoretical plot from your measurements , following the Stage 6 characteristic-length task . Use periods from 100 to 500 s, with labeled values at 100, 200, 300, 400, and 500 s. The supplied figure illustrates the quantities; your submitted curves must use your experimentally inferred parameters. Its \(q_1\) is the same mean-decay coefficient denoted by \(m\) below, and its wavelength \(2\pi/q'\) is \(2\pi\) times the one-radian phase length \(1/q'\) .

The derivation above gives only the oscillatory part. The imposed boundary temperature also has a time-independent mean: \[ T(0,t)=T_a+T_b\cos(\omega t). \] Because the rod equation is linear, add the stationary mean profile to the settled oscillatory response. For a semi-infinite rod, the complete settled-periodic solution is \[  oxed{ T(x,t)=T_\infty +(T_a-T_\infty)e^{-mx} +T_b e^{-qx}\cos(\omega t-q'x) }, \] where \[ m=\sqrt{ rac{
u}{\kappa}}, \qquad s=q+iq'=\sqrt{ rac{
u+i\omega}{\kappa}}, \qquad m^2=q^2-q'^2. \] The first exponential describes the time-averaged temperature above room temperature. The second describes the oscillation about that mean. Thus the mean and the oscillation amplitude both decrease with position, but generally with different decay coefficients, \(m\) and \(q\) . This is a settled periodic state , not a time-independent steady state. Using sine instead of cosine only changes the choice of time origin. The solution contains three physical length scales: \[ \ell_{\mathrm{mean}}= rac{1}{m}=\sqrt{ rac{\kappa}{
u}}, \qquad \ell_{\mathrm{amp}}= rac{1}{q}, \qquad \ell_{\mathrm{phase}}= rac{1}{q'}. \] Over \(\ell_{\mathrm{mean}}\) , the mean excess temperature falls by a factor of \(e\) . Over \(\ell_{\mathrm{amp}}\) , the oscillation amplitude falls by a factor of \(e\) . Over \(\ell_{\mathrm{phase}}\) , the oscillation accumulates one radian of phase lag; its spatial wavelength is therefore \(2\pi\ell_{\mathrm{phase}}\) . Define the dimensionless driving frequency \[ \Omega= rac{\omega}{
u}. \] The three scales are not independent. Dividing the relations for \(q\) and \(q'\) by \(m=\sqrt{
u/\kappa}\) gives \[  rac{q}{m} =\sqrt{ rac{1+\sqrt{1+\Omega^2}}{2}}, \qquad  rac{q'}{m} =\sqrt{ rac{-1+\sqrt{1+\Omega^2}}{2}}. \] Equivalently, \(\ell_{\mathrm{amp}}/\ell_{\mathrm{mean}}=m/q\) and \(\ell_{\mathrm{phase}}/\ell_{\mathrm{mean}}=m/q'\) . For a finite rod, the dimensionless lengths \(mL\) , \(qL\) , and \(q'L\) state how many mean-decay, amplitude-decay, and one-radian phase-shift lengths fit within the rod. Popup: Estimate The Three Dimensionless Lengths For The Course Rod Use the planning values \[ L=0.300\ \mathrm{m},\qquad d=0.0127\ \mathrm{m},\qquad h=10\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-2}K^{-1}}, \] and the 6061-T6 aluminum properties \[ k=167\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-1}K^{-1}},\qquad ho=2700\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}},\qquad c=896\ \mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}K^{-1}}. \] For a circular rod, \[ \kappa= rac{k}{ho c}=6.90	imes10^{-5}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}, \qquad 
